The  driver  supports the PCI IDE controllers as specified in the
"PCI IDE controller specification, revision 1.0" draft, and  pro‐
vides the interface with the hardware for the driver.  Please use
the  chip‐specific  drivers for the following controllers for en‐
hanced and DMA support: Acard ATP850 (Ultra/33) and  ATP860  (Ul‐
tra/66) IDE Controllers: Acer labs M5229 IDE Controller: Advanced
Micro Devices AMD‐756, 766, and 768 IDE Controllers: Advanced Mi‐
cro  Devices  Geode  IDE  Controllers: CMD Tech PCI0643, PCI0646,
PCI0648, and PCI0649 IDE Controllers: Contaq Microsystems/Cypress
CY82C693 IDE Controller: HighPoint HPT366  Ultra/66,  HPT370  Ul‐
tra/100, HPT372, and HPT374 Ultra/133 IDE controller: Intel PIIX,
PIIX3,  and  PIIX4  IDE Controllers: Intel i31244 Serial ATA con‐
troller: Intel 82801 (ICH/ICH0/ICH2/ICH3/ICH4/ICH5/ICH6) IDE Con‐
trollers: Intel SCH IDE Controllers:  NVIDIA  nForce/nForce2  IDE
Controllers: OPTi 82c621 (plus a few of its derivatives) IDE Con‐
trollers:   Promise  PDC20246  (Ultra/33),  PDC20262  (Ultra/66),
PDC20265/PDC20267  (Ultra100),  PDC20268  (Ultra/100TX2  and  Ul‐
tra/100TX2v2), Ultra/133, Ultra/133TX2 and Ultra/133TX2v2 PCI IDE
controllers:  Serverworks K2 SATA controllers: Silicon Image 0680
IDE controller: Silicon  Image  SATALink  3112  Serial  ATA  con‐
troller:  Silicon  Image  SteelVine  3124/3132/3531 Serial ATA II
controller: Silicon Integrated System 5597/5598  IDE  controller:
Symphony  Labs  82C105  and  Winbond W83C553F IDE controller: VIA
Technologies VT82C586, VT82C586A, VT82C596A, VT82C686A,  VT8233A,
and  VT8235 IDE Controllers: The 0x0001 flag forces the driver to
use DMA when there is no explicit DMA mode  setting  support  for
the controller but DMA is present.  If the BIOS didn’t set up the
controller  properly,  this can cause a machine hang.  The 0x0002
flag forces the driver to disable DMA on chipsets for  which  DMA
would  normally be enabled.  This can be used as a debugging aid,
or to work around problems where the IDE controller is  wired  up
to the system incorrectly.
